WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.030
What's a free coding resource
you could never do without?

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.280
A free coding resource that
I could never do without

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.180
is definitely GitHub,
both Git and GitHub

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.930
for keeping track of code
and managing large software

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.340
projects.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.200
Probably Jupyter Notebooks.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.110
It's an interactive sort
of console to type code

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.440
and visualize whatever
data you're working with.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.940
And it makes it really easy to
work on projects interactively

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.240
and quickly.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.510
I could probably never
do without Stack Exchange

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.130
where it's just a
lot of programmers

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.170
coming together and
giving each other answers

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.460
to their problems.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.590
Stack Overflow is incredible.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.350
I probably use it at
least 10 times a day

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:58.290
to figure out what's
[INAUDIBLE] in my program.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.470
Stack Overflow is the
most incredible website

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.313
where if you just
need to do something

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:04.980
and you have no idea
how to get started,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.030
you type it into Google.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.612
And probably the first result is
a Stack Overflow result saying,

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:11.070
this is how you
solve this problem.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.410
That, I think, has
really revolutionized

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.600
both the democratization
of coding because people

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.150
can go and figure out how to
solve their problem if they're

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.620
stuck and also has really sort
of created this great community

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.770
online where people can
ask and answer questions.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.210
Definitely Stack Overflow.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.000
I feel like if Stack
Overflow ever went down

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.150
or if they decided
to charge for it,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.620
the entire programming community
would just go up in flames.

